          "id": "104621"
        },
        "body": "@mfenniak wrote in https://code.forgejo.org/forgejo/runner/issues/1771#issuecomment-104562:\r\n\r\n> As far as I know, GitHub doesn't support this kind of thing (running a container of a different platform), so we're in an innovative spot here where it isn't clear that there's a step we can take that would help. We could try to infer the `runner.arch` from the platform option of the container, which would probably work, but might have unintended side-effects.\r\n\r\nThe problem is known. I brought I up the last time during the [development of pluggable back-ends](https://code.forgejo.org/forgejo/forgejo-actions-feature-requests/issues/107#issuecomment-92976). There's a [PR](https://code.forgejo.org/forgejo/runner/pulls/1586), but it stalled because we were unable to answer all the questions.\r\n\r\nGitHub Actions Runner runs on the same machine as the job. Most people and actions interpret `${{ runner.os }}` and `${{ runner.arch }}` as the operating system and architecture that the job is executed on. Forgejo Runner should match that. But it only does in host mode.\r\n\r\n> We could introduce new more clear variables with better definitions (...)\r\n\r\nThat's definitely needed, but it won't help with the problem at hand.\r\n\r\n> This is a pretty narrow use-case (...)\r\n\r\nUnfortunately, it no longer is since we introduced the pluggable back-end."
      },
