      "detail": {
        "type": "comment",
        "comment": {
          "forge": "forgejo",
          "instance": "code.forgejo.org",
          "thread": {
            "forge": "forgejo",
            "instance": "code.forgejo.org",
            "repo": {
              "forge": "forgejo",
              "instance": "code.forgejo.org",
              "owner": "forgejo",
              "name": "runner",
              "externalId": "2"
            },
            "kind": "issue",
            "number": "1771"
          },
          "id": "104562"
        },
        "body": "I think that the core of this problem is that Forgejo Runner's `${{ runner.arch }}` is set to `ARM64` when the runner is compiled on arm64, regardless of the platform of the images being executed.  The runner isn't putting this in the path, but I suspect one of the other actions is inferring the appropriate path to be added based upon the runner.arch.\r\n\r\nThis is consistent with [our documentation on the variable](https://forgejo.org/docs/latest/user/actions/reference/#runner) -- it is \"The CPU architecture of the **runner**; ...\"\r\n\r\nAs far as I know, GitHub doesn't support this kind of thing (running a container of a different platform), so we're in an innovative spot here where it isn't clear that there's a step we can take that would help.  We could try to infer the `runner.arch` from the platform option of the container, which would probably work, but might have unintended side-effects.  We could introduce new more clear variables with better definitions, but then GitHub's actions won't use them anyway.\r\n\r\nIf you were writing the code that was inspecting the platform yourself, I'd suggest using `uname -m` rather than `${{ runner.arch }}`.  As this seems to come from one of the external actions used, that's not much of an option unless you want to fork them.\r\n\r\nThis is a pretty narrow use-case and I'm not personally motivated to pursue a lot of work in it, but if someone has thoughts on the right direction and is willing to do implementation work, I'd be willing to collaborate in a review role."
      },
